+# TASK-260728-2spy93 review verdict — cycle 5
+
+## Verdict
+
+**CHANGES REQUESTED**. Route to `to-dev`.
+
+The cycle-4 `buildArtifactV1` repair closes the two previously demonstrated
+escapes and the direct structural variants: the definition must be an object,
+requires exactly `path`, `sha256`, and `size`, has exactly those properties,
+closes `additionalProperties`, and pins each property to the intended shared
+definition. The compiled receipt checks also reject the direct launcher,
+bundle, empty-object, missing-member, extra-member, path-traversal, unprefixed
+digest, and negative-size cases.
+
+One blocking false-accept remains in the claimed canonical-target closure.
+
+## Blocking finding
+
+### P1 — the shared artifact targets can still be widened without rejection
+
+`check_exact_property_schemas` pins the three property schemas to references
+named `portablePath`, `sha256`, and `nonNegativeSafeInteger`.
+`check_build_artifact_rejections` then samples a finite set of invalid values,
+but it does not hold those referenced definitions to their canonical shapes or
+prove that a replacement is equivalently strict.
+
+I independently ran three in-memory mutations through
+`validate_additional_driver_boundary` and through the compiled
+`build-receipt-v2` Draft 2020-12 validator:
+
+| Mutant | Boundary gate | Current receipt | Mutated receipt |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `portablePath.maxLength`: `4096` → `4097`; artifact path is 4097 `a` characters | **accepted** | rejected | **accepted** |
+| `sha256.pattern`: lowercase hex → lowercase-or-uppercase hex; digest is 64 uppercase `A` characters | **accepted** | rejected | **accepted** |
+| `nonNegativeSafeInteger.maximum`: `9007199254740991` → `9007199254740992`; size is `9007199254740992` | **accepted** | rejected | **accepted** |
+
+Each mutation leaves the property `$ref` unchanged and continues to reject the
+finite negative examples currently generated by
+`rejected_artifact_instances`, so the new gate returns successfully. Each
+nevertheless broadens what the actual receipt validator accepts.
+
+This contradicts:
+
+- the cycle-4 rework requirement that each property retain the canonical shared
+  definition or an equivalently strict compiled-schema contract;
+- decision 0008 section 11's claim that the compiled receipt proof keeps the
+  three referenced definitions from being widened underneath the pinned
+  references; and
+- the `native-executable-v1` identity boundary, whose path, digest, and size
+  semantics are part of the artifact identity rather than illustrative samples.
+
+## Required rework and re-review gate
+
+Keep the accepted cycle-1 through cycle-4 repairs and the exact three-file
+scope. Close this one gap by doing one of the following:
+
+1. structurally pin the exact current schemas of `portablePath`, `sha256`, and
+   `nonNegativeSafeInteger` in the boundary gate; or
+2. implement a complete, defensible equivalence/subsumption check that proves a
+   changed target is no less strict than the canonical contract.
+
+The first option is recommended because these are small closed schemas and is
+deterministic and auditable.
+
+Add red regressions for at least the three accepted mutants above, plus the
+existing coarse widened-target cases. Re-review must show that the boundary
+gate rejects all three while baseline receipt positives still validate and all
+existing direct artifact mutants remain closed.
+
+## Independent verification evidence
+
